# November 30, 2024

### Korean War Museum

Around this time, we had the opportunity to go to Korea while visiting family. After having some incredible food at an outdoor market (the name of which I cannot remember), including chijimi and a rice flour donut, we separated into groups. My wife, sister-in-law, and mother-in-law took my son and went shopping (Korean fashion is very popular in Japan), and my brother-in-law came with me and my daughter to the Korean War Museum.

In my experience, most of us Americans are familiar with the Korean War in the 50s due to our involvement with it, but this museum provided: information; showed artifacts, videos, and testimonials; and even replicas of structures and tools used in the various conflicts that have been recorded in what is now the Korean peninsula. For example, I was surprised to know that in the 1800s America had a small conflict with Korea caused by a missing merchant ship and a series of misunderstandings which lead to bloodshed. Eventually the conflict was resolved after a reported 256 people died, and many were injured.

Many more snippets and stories like this existed, and much of the museum was dedicated to more modern wars, such as the Imperial Japanese occupation after the Russo-Japanese War, or the Korean war that happened more recently as I mentioned above. Ultimately, I took away the impression that Korea has been part of many, many wars in the last few centuries, often due to the influence and decisions made by other, nearby countries seeking to expand their political, economic, or military standing. I certainly felt a large share of pity and sympathy for the Korean people, many of whom suffered unnecessarily through the years. It made me wonder what sort of influence all of these conflicts inevitably had on what I saw as a very happy, vibrant, and faceted culture.

### Gaming Cafe

On a completely different topic, I was finally able to experience a slice of Korean cult7ure that I've been interested in for quite a while -- ever since Starcraft II came out and I started to get into the competitive side of the game. The game came out in the summer of 2010, I believe, when I was a young buck of 13 years old. I was just terrible at the game for a long while, but I eventually learned about something called "build orders", which is a term that just means playing the game around a specific structure, and my ability to compete at a higher level skyrocketed. I got absurdly into Starcraft II while I was in college. After letting it go for a few years, I got back into it recently as an adult (albiet far less competitively), and was able to go to a PC cafe in Korea and play a few games of Starcraft in the Asian region. I had a lot of fun.
